{
  "_comment": "Two families. Ballistic is cheap sustained single-target damage; Cannon lobs shells that damage everything inside a radius, falling off to splash_min_fraction at the edge. Costs and the ~2.2x DPS-per-tier ladder follow master plan section 3.2. Cannon deliberately has lower single-target DPS, a slower rate of fire and a shorter range than Ballistic at the same tier - it is paid for in area, and against one heavy target it is the wrong tool. Missile/Energy/Support are P1. Arc Suppressor is the third family and the first that is not primarily about damage: it does a fraction of what a Ballistic does and instead drags everything in a small blast radius down to a fraction of its speed. That is a force multiplier, not a killer - every other turret on the board gets more time on target - which makes it the direct answer to the Vanguard Lance, whose whole threat is that it crosses a firing arc too fast to be killed in it. Slows refresh rather than stack (see Sim._suppress), so a wall of Suppressors cannot pin a wave in place. Railgun is the fourth family and the answer to the Siege Breaker: enormous single-shot damage on a very slow cycle, the longest range in the game, and a round that does not stop at the first thing it hits - it damages everything within a lane of its flight path, at full damage the whole way. That makes it worth its cost against a column on a straight and close to worthless against a scattered swarm, which is the trade it is meant to present.",
